In Nextflow, you can access and manipulate parameters and strings using Groovy language features. If you want to take the `params.group`, which is a string that looks like "m1.txt", and split it to obtain the "m1" part, you can use the `split` method and access the first element of the resulting array.

Here is how you can modify the script to achieve this:

```groovy
workflow {
  dir = params.dir
  meta = params.meta
  group = params.group
  name = params.group.split('\\.')[0] // Split the string by dot and take the first element
  // ... rest of your workflow ...
}
```

In this modification, `split('\\.')` is used to divide the string `params.group` at every occurrence of a period (`.`). Since the dot is a special character in regular expressions, it needs to be escaped with two backslashes (`\\`). The `split` method returns an array, and by accessing the element `[0]`, you obtain the first portion of the string, which is "m1" in this case.

Remember that you can also define this kind of logic outside the workflow block if you need to use it in different scopes or for clarity:

```groovy
// Define a function to extract the name
def extractName(group) {
  return group.split('\\.')[0]
}

workflow {
  dir = params.dir
  meta = params.meta
  group = params.group
  name = extractName(params.group)
  // ... rest of your workflow ...
}
```

Both approaches will store "m1" into the `name` variable, as you desired.

