The error you're encountering is due to Rust's strict borrowing rules. Specifically, you can't have more than one mutable reference to the same data at a time for safe concurrency, and closures capture variables from their environment, which can sometimes lead to borrowing issues.

The closure used in the `for_each` method with `par_iter_mut()` has mutable access to the elements of `chr_acc`, but inside that closure, you are also trying to push to `chr_acc`, which requires a separate mutable access and violates Rust's borrowing rules. That's why the compiler is complaining.

To resolve this, you'll need to find a way to avoid borrowing `chr_acc` mutably more than once.

One possible solution is to collect the new elements you want to add to `chr_acc` in a separate `Vec`, and then extend `chr_acc` after the `for_each` block. This way you can avoid the double mutable borrowing.

Here's how you might go about fixing the code:

```rust
// This variable will hold the new elements you want to add to `chr_acc`
let mut new_elements = vec![];

chr_acc.par_iter_mut().for_each(|x| {
    if x.2 == gene {
        x.0 = std::cmp::min(x.0, record.tx_start);
        x.1 = std::cmp::max(x.1, record.tx_end);
    } else {
        // Instead of pushing to `chr_acc` here, we push to `new_elements`
        new_elements.push((record.tx_start, record.tx_end, gene.clone()));
    }
});

// Now that the `for_each` block is over, we can safely extend `chr_acc`
chr_acc.extend(new_elements);
```

This pattern of collecting then extending is common in Rust to avoid mutable aliasing.

