Using a `Mutex` is a simple and straightforward way to ensure safe concurrent access to shared data, but it is not always the fastest or most efficient due to the overhead of lock management and potential contention.

Rayon provides a method, `fold_with`, which is designed for use cases like this. It allows each thread to accumulate results independently into a thread-local copy of some initial value and then combine all the results at the end. This is usually much faster than locking because it avoids contention between threads.

Here's a revised version of the code using `fold_with`:

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;
use std::collections::HashMap;

pub fn parse_tracks<'a>(
    contents: &'a str,
) -> Result<
    (
        HashMap<String, Record>,
        HashMap<String, Vec<(u32, u32, String)>>,
    ),
    &'static str,
> {
    // We use a thread-local HashMap to accumulate results for `pseudomap`.
    let (tracks, pseudomaps) = contents
        .par_lines()
        .filter(|x| !x.starts_with("#"))
        .filter_map(|x| Record::new(x).ok())
        .fold_with((HashMap::new(), HashMap::new()), |(mut tracks, mut pseudomap), record| {
            let ps_acc = pseudomap.entry(record.chrom.clone()).or_insert_with(Vec::new);
            ps_acc.push((record.tx_start, record.tx_end, record.id.clone()));

            tracks.entry(record.id.clone()).or_insert(record);
            (tracks, pseudomap)
        })
        .reduce(
            || (HashMap::new(), HashMap::new()),
            |(mut tracks1, mut pseudomap1), (mut tracks2, mut pseudomap2)| {
                tracks1.extend(tracks2);
                for (chrom, mut positions) in pseudomap2.drain() {
                    pseudomap1.entry(chrom).or_insert_with(Vec::new).append(&mut positions);
                }
                (tracks1, pseudomap1)
            },
        );

    Ok((tracks, pseudomaps))
}
```

