The error "Value must be numeric and finite" in the `rectangle` function is related to the inputs for the position argument. It seems that one or more of the input values for the `[x, y, w, h]` rectangle position vector are not numeric or finite.

One way to fix this issue is to check that all the values used in the `rectangle` function are valid. For example, you can add some `if` statements to ensure that the position values are numeric and finite before calling the function. Here's an updated version of the loop code with these modifications:

```
for j = 1:N_cars
    x_rect = x_prev(j)-L(j)/2;
    y_rect = (j-1)*2;
    w_rect = L(j);
    h_rect = 2;
    if all(isfinite([x_rect, y_rect, w_rect, h_rect]))
        rectangle('Position',[x_rect, y_rect, w_rect, h_rect],'FaceColor',colors(j,:),'Curvature',j/N_cars);
    else
        warning('Invalid rectangle position values for car %d.', j);
    end
end
```

This code checks that the position values `[x_rect, y_rect, w_rect, h_rect]` for each rectangle are all finite and numeric before calling the `rectangle` function. If any of these values is not valid (which could happen if the simulation goes awry), it will raise a warning but continue to the next rectangle.

